course: world
lesson: 127
title: Sleep and the body clock
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Your body has its own clock, called the body clock or circadian rhythm ("about a day"), which runs on about 24 hours. Morning light sets it each day, and getting up at the same time trains it, which is why Mia's body wakes her at 7 even on Saturdays with no alarm.
explain: Sleep is not wasted time: while you sleep, your growing body and brain do important work, sorting what you learned and repairing. Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours a night, teenagers about 8 to 10, adults about 7 to 9, and newborn babies 14 to 17 hours, in short pieces.
explain: To choose a bedtime, count back from your wake-up time: up at 7 am and needing 10 hours means asleep by 9 pm. Bright screens in bed tell the brain "it's still day", so it makes less melatonin, the sleep chemical, and next day you're dead on your feet.
explain: In teenagers the body clock shifts later, so they feel sleepy late and hate mornings: partly biology, not just laziness. Fly across time zones and your body clock stays at home; that's jet lag, and flying east is often harder, because you must sleep earlier than your body wants.
explain: Almost all living things keep time like this, even flowers that open in the morning and close in the evening. Scientists who found out how body clocks work won the Nobel Prize in 2017.
fact: The body clock, or circadian rhythm, runs on about 24 hours, matching one spin of the Earth.
fact: Morning light sets the body clock, and getting up at the same time each day trains it.
fact: Children aged 6 to 12 need about 9 to 12 hours of sleep a night; adults need about 7 to 9.
fact: Teenagers need about 8 to 10 hours, but their body clock shifts later, so they feel sleepy later at night.
fact: Newborn babies sleep about 14 to 17 hours a day, waking every 2 to 3 hours to feed.
fact: Bright screens at night make the brain release less melatonin, the body chemical that helps sleep.
fact: Sleep from 8:30 pm to 7:00 am is 10 hours 30 minutes; to sleep 10 hours and wake at 7 am, you must fall asleep at 9 pm.
fact: Too little sleep makes it harder to concentrate, learn and stay in a good mood.
fact: Jet lag happens when the body clock doesn't match the local clock after flying across time zones; flying east is often harder.
fact: Los Angeles is 8 hours behind London, so 3 pm in Los Angeles is 11 pm in London.
fact: Tokyo is 9 hours ahead of London in British winter, so 3 am in London is noon in Tokyo.
fact: Missing a whole night's sleep makes people slow, grumpy and clumsy, and lost sleep can't really be caught up in one go.
fact: Almost all living things have body clocks, even plants and bacteria; scientists who found how they work won the Nobel Prize in 2017.
exercise: About how long does the body clock take to run once round? | About 24 hours | About 12 hours | About 1 week | About 1 hour
exercise: What sets the body clock each morning? | Light | The smell of breakfast | The sound of the alarm | Brushing your teeth
exercise: How much sleep does a 10-year-old need each night? | About 9 to 12 hours | About 6 hours | About 4 to 5 hours | About 15 to 17 hours
exercise: Ruby goes to bed at 8:30 pm and gets up at 7:00 am. How long is that? | 10 hours 30 minutes | 11 hours 30 minutes | 9 hours 30 minutes | 1 hour 30 minutes
exercise: Jake wakes at 7 am and needs 10 hours' sleep. When must he fall asleep? | 9 pm | 10 pm | 8 pm | 5 pm
exercise: Lexi sleeps from 10:30 pm to 7:00 am. How long is that? | 8 hours 30 minutes | 9 hours 30 minutes | 7 hours 30 minutes | 3 hours 30 minutes
exercise: Why do screens in bed make it harder to fall asleep? | Bright light makes the brain release less melatonin | Screens make the room far too warm to fall asleep in | Screens use up all of the body's energy for the night | Screens make your eyes much too wet and blurry to close
exercise: Why does Mia wake at 7 on Saturdays with no alarm? | Her body clock is trained by getting up at the same time | Saturdays have more light in the morning than any other day | Her body knows the names of the days | She is ill and can't sleep
exercise: How much sleep do teenagers need? | About 8 to 10 hours | About 4 to 6 hours | About 12 to 14 hours | About 6 hours, like adults
exercise: Why do many teenagers feel sleepy late at night? | Their body clock shifts later | They are lazier than children | They drink more milk | Their eyes get bigger at night
exercise: What is jet lag? | When the body clock doesn't match the local clock after a flight | Feeling sick from the shaking of a plane | Waiting a long time for a late plane | The loud noise of a jet engine that stays ringing in your ears for days
exercise: It is 3 pm in Los Angeles, 8 hours behind London. What time does Ivy's body think it is? | About 11 pm | About 7 am | About 3 pm | About 5 pm
exercise: It is 3 am in London in winter. What time is it in Tokyo, 9 hours ahead? | 12 noon | 6 pm | 6 am | 3 am
exercise: Which way of flying is often harder for jet lag? | East | West | North | South
exercise: How much do newborn babies sleep? | About 14 to 17 hours a day | About 8 hours a day | About 20 to 23 hours a day | About 3 to 4 hours a day
exercise: Which living things have body clocks? | Almost all, even plants and bacteria | Only people | Only animals with brains | Only birds and bats
test: Ollie: "I'm 10, I only need 6 hours' kip like Dad." ("Kip" means sleep.) Mum: "Not a chance, sunshine." Why is Mum right? | Children need about 9 to 12 hours, more than adults | Children need exactly 6 hours, just the same as adults | Children need 6 hours on school nights, more at weekends | Adults actually need a lot more sleep than children do
test: Lexi plays on her tablet in bed till half ten, then she's "dead on her feet" at school. What does that mean, and why? | Very tired: too little sleep, and the screen delayed it | Very ill: tablets give you a fever if you use them in bed at night | Very bored: school is dull after games | Very cold: she forgot her coat
test: Mia: "Saturday, I could have a lie-in, but my body's like nope." (A lie-in is sleeping late.) What's happening? | Her body clock wakes her at the usual time | Her body clock is broken and needs to be fixed | She isn't tired at all because it's the weekend | Her body runs on a 7-day clock, not a daily one
test: Big brother Sam, 16, can't sleep before midnight. Dad: "Lazy." Mum: "It's his age." Who has the science on her side? | Mum: teenage body clocks shift later | Dad: teenagers just choose to be lazy | Dad: teenagers need only about 5 hours | Neither: teenagers have no body clock
test: Ivy, in LA at 3 pm, nodding off over her burger: "This is so mid." ("Mid" means rubbish.) Why's she so sleepy? | Her body still thinks it's 11 pm London time | She ate too much American food | LA air has less oxygen than London | The flight made her lose a whole night
test: Kai's dad, wide awake at 3 am after Tokyo: "Jet lag's a nightmare, mate." What time does his body think it is? | About noon, Tokyo time | About 3 am, like London | About 6 pm, Tokyo time | About midnight, Tokyo time
test: Ella: "Babies sleep 15 hours? Lucky." If a baby sleeps 15 hours a day, how long is it awake? | About 9 hours, in short bits | About 15 hours in one go | About 1 hour | About 24 hours
test: Zane, after an all-nighter at a sleepover: "Never again, bruv." He's grumpy for two days. What does this show? | One lost night can cost two days; sleep can't be caught up at once | All-nighters make you stronger | Sleep can be saved up before a sleepover | Grumpiness comes from too much sleep
test: What would happen if you lived in a cave with no clocks and no daylight for weeks? | Your body clock would keep running on about 24 hours | You would fall asleep and wake up at totally random times | You would stop sleeping altogether | Your body would switch to a 12-hour day
test: Mila: "Do flowers have body clocks, miss?" What's the answer? | Yes, some open in the morning and close in the evening | No, only animals with brains do | No, plants never keep time | Yes, but only roses
test: Jake's mate from Texas: "Y'all go to bed at 9? I'm fixin' to stay up all night!" ("Fixin' to" means about to.) Jake wakes at 7 and needs 10 hours. Who's sensible? | Jake: asleep by 9 pm gives him his 10 hours | The mate: staying up all night is really healthy | Neither: 10-year-olds need only about 6 hours | The mate: sleep only really matters for babies
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the circadian rhythm? | An internal cycle of approximately twenty-four hours, reset chiefly by light | An internal cycle of approximately seven days, reset by meals | An external cycle imposed by the striking of clocks | A cycle observable exclusively in human beings
exam: Given that a child retires to sleep at 8:30 pm and rises at 7:00 am, ascertain the duration of sleep obtained. | Ten hours and thirty minutes | Eleven hours and thirty minutes | Nine hours and thirty minutes | Ten hours exactly
exam: Identify the principal reason for which the use of bright screens before sleep delays its onset. | The light diminishes the release of melatonin | The light increases the temperature of the bedroom | The screens deplete the body's reserves of energy | The screens emit sounds inaudible to the user
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the recommended duration of sleep for children aged six to twelve years. | Approximately nine to twelve hours nightly | Approximately six hours nightly, as for adults | Approximately fourteen to seventeen hours nightly | Approximately four to five hours nightly
